00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.550 align:start position:0%
 
hello everyone  so uh every year here at iit kharagpur  and i'm sure in many of the other iits  also we have this very important piece  of evaluation for the final year  students called the comprehensive viva  now there is a grand event uh  and it's something which often induces a  lot of fear among the students and i  have been  a part of this comprehensive viva both  as a student and as a faculty member  for the past few years so i thought that  i'll  share a few tips with you guys  um  before i uh go on to the tips uh  let me just clarify and uh gives a bit  of disclaimer that in no way my comments  should be taken as the official comments  of the mechanical engineering department  of which i'm a  faculty member nor of the institute okay  these are my personal views alone  something which i have  garnered  as a student  and as a faculty member  all right so i've written down these  points because i have a tendency to rant  without proper notes so  some very common mystic students make  the first and foremost is that  is a mistake they make even before  coming to the viva and that is they  don't prepare well enough now this goes  without saying that this is a very  important piece of evaluation and they  should be preparing well but what i mean  to say is that  uh  well uh let's put this in perspective  you see uh this viva takes place in the  final year so  basically the students are officially  supposed to be uh  to be knowing something like 30 to 40  subjects  well  humanly it is not possible to remember  everything  okay i am a faculty member  i don't remember many of the things  which i teach myself every semester okay  and how am i going to expect students to  remember everything  from those 30 40 subjects it's not  possible so  the most strategic thing that a student  can do  is to prepare well to their strengths  now this is something which almost every  student fails to do they do not  prepare to their strengths  what i mean by that is that every  student has a certain bent of mind and  depending on that they are good in  certain subjects  they should  properly and very strongly revise those  subjects okay suppose someone is very  good in solid mechanics or in fluid  mechanics or  maybe in some subjects of manufacturing  maybe casting forming welding  or something else internal combustion  engines whatever  they should prepare that really really  really well  okay  now  uh  here in  iit kharagpur  in the mechanical engineering department  we have  uh three sections  uh one is manufacturing  one is uh thermal science and  engineering and the other is mechanical  systems design  now  the panels that are made for these  comprehensive fibers consisting of maybe  four five six professors  uh  assigned to each of these panels they  will be like  eight to ten students maybe a little bit  more sometimes  uh so these five or six professors or  maybe four or five professors they will  be evaluating these nine or ten students  now  the way that these panels are  constituted is that  there will be a good balance between the  various among the various sections so  there may be two professors from  manufacturing to professors from thermal  science and engineering and two  professors from  uh mechanical systems design  so that there's a good mixture of  questions that is asked to the students  accordingly it is expected  although it is not officially written  down anywhere that the students  would have prepared  well officially the expectation is that  they would have prepared everything but  as i said humanly that is not possible  so  uh it is at least expected that they  would know something from  a few subjects from each of these  sections  okay so for example when they come  for example this just a as a just a case  that they may have studied something  from casting forming welding and  maybe modern methods in manufacturing  and then from the thermal side maybe  fluid mechanics and thermodynamics and  maybe from  the mechanical system design may be  dynamics and mechanics of solids or some  other combination  now  what happens is that students are so  much overall by the this this huge list  of subjects  that  they sometimes do not  uh  revise at all from a certain section  okay maybe they completely uh overlook  the mechanical system design part or  maybe they completely overlook the  manufacturing side now that's a very  very bad mistake to make okay and it  kind of pisses off some of the  professors who are there in the panel  on the flip side okay so this is one  mistake there's a mistake on the flip  side to it also  sometimes what students do  rather mischievously is that  some sometimes they get to know uh  who which uh which of the professors  will be there in that panel and they try  to kind of second guess  the nature of the questions which will  be asked now this is rather mysterious  okay  i came across this instance of a very  mischievous student who  uh  uh who said that he had really studied  well from a particular topic  and  that topic or that subject  was not taught  by any of the panel members sitting in  that panel including me  so  it was kind of awkward but then  well professors can also be a little bit  mischievous so what we did was that we  asked him  not exactly on that topic because none  of us were teaching that's a particular  thing  so we asked something very basic which  is that the fundamentals of that topic  the student claimed to be knowing  and it just turned out that he didn't  know even that so he ended up getting  very bad marks  so mischievous  mischief can work both ways  now  on a more serious note  students should not try to second guess  what kind of  questions will be asked in a particular  panel rather as i said they should  prepare to their strengths  maybe study properly two or three  subjects from each of these sections  from manufacturing thermal science  mechanical systems design and that way  they will be able to answer a wide  variety of questions  that are asked in the panel  so this is one one aspect  so  another mistake which  students make  is that  they kind of get so nervous that they  very easily give it up  so usually we do not ask questions  which  which are formula based or  which asks you to remember a certain  equation although we can ask some  questions like that but generally we  don't we avoid asking questions like  that rather  what we ask is based on the fundamental  concepts  we will ask a question which will uh  which will  test the student  to apply that concept the general  notions of that concept to a particular  application  so uh  maybe we ask something like  uh  if you want to construct a beam uh  that is that has to be of a rectangular  cross section then uh  how should you place the beam  so the longer side should be vertical or  the longer side should be horizontal  so things like that and that can be  easily answered by  having a very preliminary understanding  of how the second moment of area or the  moment of inertia is calculated  okay  i think i think this is  i mean there are certain kinds of  students who  because of the lack of revision  sometimes or because  they are a little bit too nervous  they  don't try to apply their brain in front  of the panel to try to answer these  questions  and i'm quite confident that if they  were asked the same question maybe over  a cup of tea when they were very much  relaxed they would be  immediately be able to figure the figure  out the answer so uh although it's  easier said than done yeah my advice is  that try to relax a little bit okay we  are not trying to  uh we are not trying to  kind of  extract what you don't know  rather we are trying to extract the best  of what you know  uh again  on the flip side of it  there are certain students  who  will try to [ __ ] their way out of  the of the question so when they don't  know  they will just try to [ __ ] their way  okay now this is the worst mistake that  a student can make  okay and uh  i have uh kind of  themed this  video like  common mistakes that the riotians make  during comprehensive survivors  but this applies equally well to any any  viva for any student whether in iits or  any other institute okay students should  never try to [ __ ] their way out of  the question if you don't know the  answer to a question to a technical  question  say that you don't know okay it's much  much better that way  okay  say you you have not studied it or maybe  you have completely forgotten it you  have no here you do not recall that this  concept at all  come come clean with it okay please do  not try to [ __ ] this is the worst  offense  uh  so  uh i think these are these are the main  things that i wanted to talk about um  i'll just end up with one little  anecdote that i have from my own student  days so this was back in 2008  uh  so i was sitting in my comprehensive  viva fourth year  and  i was asked a question on  some topic of manufacturing i think it  was  something regarding the importance of  riser  and i  mentioned a few points on the importance  of ryzen and then  i kind of lost my way and said something  uh and then one of the professors in the  panel he kind of  very  mischievously said that  evan jyoti i think you have done a very  good decision by changing your dual  degree specialization from manufacturing  to mechanical systems design  well  uh it just so happens that  uh  in a few days from now maybe  so today is sunday on wednesday i am  going to sit in a panel for  comprehensive  along with the same professor  on this side of the table  such as life  thank you

